 ## Overview
-Gaussian Texture Filtering via Jacobian Back-projection is a technique to compute the color of a screen pixel by integrating a texture signal against a Gaussian filter, where the filter is back-projected from screen space to texture space using the local affine approximation (Jacobian) of the texture mapping [S1, S2]. It solves the problem of aliasing in texture mapping by ensuring that the frequency content of the projected texture is properly band-limited before sampling [S1, S2]. Unlike sibling methods that may rely on pre-filtered mipmaps or simple box filters, this method explicitly models the pixel footprint as a Gaussian and adapts its shape in texture space based on the local distortion of the surface mapping [S1, S2].
+Gaussian Texture Filtering via Jacobian Back-projection computes the color of a screen pixel by integrating a texture signal against a Gaussian filter, where the filter is back-projected from screen space to texture space using the local affine approximation (Jacobian) of the texture mapping [S1, S2]. It solves the problem of aliasing in texture mapping by explicitly modeling the pixel footprint as a Gaussian and adapting its shape in texture space based on the local distortion of the surface mapping [S1, S2]. Unlike sibling methods that rely on pre-filtered mipmaps or simple box filters, this method evaluates the convolution between the screen-space filter and the projected texture directly in the texture domain [S1, S2].
 
